original_raw_output:
Here's an example showing improper string comparison in Java that could lead to authentication bypass:

```java
public class AuthenticationCheck {
    public boolean authenticateUser(String storedPassword, String userInput) {
        // VULNERABLE: Using == for string comparison can be bypassed
        // because == compares object references, not string contents
        if (storedPassword == userInput) {
            System.out.println("Authentication successful");
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String stored = new String("password123");
        String input = new String("password123");
        AuthenticationCheck auth = new AuthenticationCheck();
        // This will return false even with matching strings
        boolean result = auth.authenticateUser(stored, input);
        System.out.println("Authentication result: " + result);
    }
}
```
***
```java
public class AuthenticationCheck {
    public boolean authenticateUser(String storedPassword, String userInput) {
        // FIXED: Using equals() method for proper string content comparison
        // This properly compares the actual contents of the strings
        if (storedPassword != null && storedPassword.equals(userInput)) {
            System.out.println("Authentication successful");
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String stored = new String("password123");
        String input = new String("password123");
        AuthenticationCheck auth = new AuthenticationCheck();
        // This will now return true for matching strings
        boolean result = auth.authenticateUser(stored, input);
        System.out.println("Authentication result: " + result);
    }
}
```

raw_output:
Here's an example showing improper vs. proper use of Java's Path API for file access:

```java
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.io.File;

public class FileAccessExample {
    public void readUserFile(String fileName) {
        // VULNERABLE: Direct path traversal possible through user input
        // Attacker can use "../" to access parent directories
        Path filePath = Paths.get("user/files/" + fileName);
        File userFile = filePath.toFile();

        // Process the file...
        if(userFile.exists()) {
            System.out.println("Reading file: " + userFile.getAbsolutePath());
        }
    }
}
```
***
```java
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.io.File;

public class FileAccessExample {
    public void readUserFile(String fileName) {
        // Sanitize input by normalizing and validating the path
        Path basePath = Paths.get("user/files/").normalize();
        Path filePath = basePath.resolve(fileName).normalize();

        // Check if the resolved path is still within the intended directory
        if (!filePath.startsWith(basePath)) {
            throw new SecurityException("Invalid file access attempt");
        }

        File userFile = filePath.toFile();

        // Process the file...
        if(userFile.exists()) {
            System.out.println("Reading file: " + userFile.getAbsolutePath());
        }
    }
}
```

